<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  The Lockheed Martin F-35B Lightning II's published figures, and the
  conditions each was measured in, so far as they are given.

  From Lockheed Martin's F-35B product card (C2023, 23-08442_002, PIRA
  AER2023060207) and its F-35 Lightning II Program Status and Fast Facts
  (April 2020, FG19-24749_004). Its performance is mostly not public: its
  maximum Mach is given at no altitude, its range on no profile. The checks in
  src/sim/figures.cpp fly one flight per <figure> and compare what JSBSim does
  with the range given here; the tolerances are this project's.

  **There is no ceiling figure here, and that is deliberate.** Lockheed
  publishes no service ceiling for any F-35, and the "above 50,000 feet" the
  F-35A was held to is the Air Force's fact sheet, for the A alone. Nothing
  published gives the F-35B one, so none is claimed.

  **The lift fan is not modelled** (tools/make_f35b.py), so none of the STOVL
  figures - hover thrust, vertical landing, short take-off - can be checked
  and none is listed.
-->
<published_figures aircraft="f35b" flaps_full_deg="0">
  <source>Lockheed Martin F-35B product card (2023); Lockheed Martin F-35 Fast Facts (April 2020)</source>

  <!-- 39,750 lb: the empty weight, 32,300 lb (Fast Facts); the pilot, 230
       lb; two AIM-120s internally, 670 lb at 335 lb each (the Air Force's
       AMRAAM fact sheet) - the F-35B's weapon bays are shorter than the
       F-35A's and this project does not claim more than two; and half the
       13,100 lb of internal fuel (product card). -->
  <loading name="combat" total_lbs="39750">
    <pointmass index="0" lbs="230"/>
    <pointmass index="1" lbs="670"/>
    <tank index="0" lbs="3275"/>
    <tank index="1" lbs="3275"/>
  </loading>

  <!-- 46,300 lb: the same with its tanks full. -->
  <loading name="full" total_lbs="46300">
    <pointmass index="0" lbs="230"/>
    <pointmass index="1" lbs="670"/>
    <tank index="0" lbs="6550"/>
    <tank index="1" lbs="6550"/>
  </loading>

  <figure name="maximum_mach" fuel_frozen="1" flight="best_level_mach" loading="combat"
          unit="Mach" value="1.6" tolerance="0.05" altitude_ft="30000" altitude_to_ft="50000"
          mach="1.3">
    The product card's "Speed Mach 1.6"; Fast Facts gives Mach 1.6 "with full
    internal weapons load". No altitude is given; flown level at every 2,000
    ft from 30,000 to 50,000 ft at full throttle, the fastest.
  </figure>

  <figure name="range" flight="cruise_range" loading="full" unit="nmi" min="900" max="1500"
          altitude_ft="40000" mach="0.8">
    The product card's "Range (Internal Fuel) >900 n.mi/1,667 km"; Fast Facts
    gives the same on the USMC profile. The profile is not published; flown
    level at 40,000 ft and Mach 0.8, this project's choice, to the last of its
    fuel with none kept back, so further than a profile with reserves goes.
    1,500 nm bounds a model gone wrong.
  </figure>
</published_figures>
